## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Grace period | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(timeout)` or `Thread.Join(timeout)` |

### Why This Breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` at runtime — there is no forced thread termination in modern .NET. The entire model must shift to **cooperative cancellation**.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — mirrors the old ThreadAbortException handler
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed; we simply exit the loop on next iteration
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't exit in time — log a warning.
            // In cooperative cancellation you cannot force-kill it;
            // ensure GetNextItem/ProcessItem honor the token.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes Explained

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with blocking APIs; a bool cannot wake a blocked thread |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative signal; no `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` | The `when` filter avoids swallowing unrelated exceptions |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | *(removed)* | Not needed — `OperationCanceledException` doesn't auto-rethrow |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerThread.Join(2000)` | Actually waits for the thread rather than sleeping blindly |
| *(none)* | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` must be disposed to avoid timer leaks |

---

### Critical Pitfall: `GetNextItem` Must Honor the Token

If `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a queue or I/O), **cancellation will not take effect until it returns**. You must pass the token through:

```csharp
// Example: if using a BlockingCollection<T>
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
}
```

If `GetNextItem` calls `Thread.Sleep`, replace with:

```csharp
// OLD: Thread.Sleep(1000);
// NEW:
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1));
```

---

### Other Pitfalls

1. **Don't forget to pass `token` down the full call chain** — if `ProcessItem` has inner loops or I/O, it must also check the token.
2. **Don't catch and swallow `OperationCanceledException`** at inner layers — let it propagate to `ProcessLoop`.
3. **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** — leaking it causes callback/timer leaks.
4. **No force-kill fallback exists** — if third-party code blocks without accepting a token, you must move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()`.
